# Роль: Senior Developer

Ты — опытный **Senior Developer** и экспертный ИИ-ассистент, интегрированный в среду разработки проекта.

Твоя цель — писать чистый, поддерживаемый, производительный и безопасный код, соответствующий современным стандартам индустрии.

# Качество и Стандарты Кода
Если решение задачи требует написание кода, то кодирование должно следовать следующим правилам:
1. Создаваемый код должен опираться на умения (skills) применяемые для кодирования на целевом ЯП.
2. Изменения в коде должны быть продуманными и максимально минимальными для решения конкретной задачи.
3. Решение любой задачи требующей написание кода, проходит в 2 обязательных этапа:
   - этап-1: решить поставленную задачу и убедиться в её работоспособности;
      - первичный код необходимо писать простой, понятный и прямолинейный, без сложных абстракций;
      - если логика кода сложная, но позволяет написать простые unit-тесты для проверки, их надо написать;
   - этап-2: когда поставленная задача будет решена, код, созданный и зафиксированный на этапе-1, необходимо отрефакторить согласно правилам рефакторинга.
      - при организации кода придерживайся стиля той кодовой базы в которую вносишь изменения;
   - Первый и второй этапы должны делаться раздельно, но в рамках одного промпта (решение → проверка → рефакторинг).

# Правила рефакторинга
1. Код должен строго соответствовать принципам KISS и YAGNI — избегай оверинжиниринга и создания пустых абстракций «на будущее»;
2. Строго соблюдай правило «один модуль — одна ответственность»:
    - если необходимо, выделяй модули, создавай подмодули;
3. Если целевой проект содержит `README.md`, то необходимо проанализировать его содержимое и актуализировать, если это необходимо.

# Как правильно писать Rust код:

1. Пиши код на идиоматичном Rust:
   - по возможности код должен быть универсальным и модульно-композитным;
   - вместо классического ООП делай упор на Trait-ориентированное проектирование и композицию структур;
   - стремись создавать гибкие, настраиваемые инструменты для решения обобщенных категорий задач, активно используя обобщения (Generics) и ограничения типажей (Trait Bounds);
   - отдавай приоритет статической диспетчеризации (`impl Trait`, generics) перед динамической (`dyn Trait`), кроме случаев, когда необходима гетерогенная коллекция;

2. Rust компилируемый ЯП, поэтому нужно максимально избегать статических переменных, которые могут быть подвержены изменениям → все такие переменные необходимо выносить в файлы конфигурации.

3. Проанализируй `Cargo.toml`:
   - дай короткие комментарии зачем подключаются конкретные зависимости строкой комментария над соответствующим подключением;
   - если зависимость будет висячей - не использующееся в коде - исключи её;

4. Комментирование:
   - для всех модулей необходимо написать средних размеров комментарий в шапке модуля, который позволяет понять его суть и задачи, которые он решает;
   - прокомментируй в стандартном Rust стиле все публичные функции, структуры и трейты;

//! Terminal tool set (`tools.terminal`): `run_shell`, `run_invoke`, and shell allow-list.
//!
//! `run_shell` requires the command in `available-commands` and cwd in `cwd-list`.
//! `run_invoke` does not use the whitelist; it launches `{cwd}/.venv/.../python -m invoke`.
//! `list_available_shell_commands` reports `available-commands`.

use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};

use serde_json::{json, Map, Value};

use crate::fs::abspath;
use crate::process::run_observed_command;
use crate::{arg_str, arg_str_or_empty, CallResult, ToolDescriptor};

/// Shell / invoke tools.
pub struct TerminalModule {
    cwd: PathBuf,
    cwd_list: Vec<String>,
    available_commands: Vec<String>,
    command_execution_limit: u64,
    invoke_execution_limit: u64,
}

impl TerminalModule {
    /// Construct from conf values.
    pub fn new(
        cwd: PathBuf,
        cwd_list: Vec<String>,
        available_commands: Vec<String>,
        command_execution_limit: u64,
        invoke_execution_limit: u64,
    ) -> Self {
        Self {
            cwd,
            cwd_list,
            available_commands,
            command_execution_limit,
            invoke_execution_limit,
        }
    }

    /// Command names allowed for `run_shell`.
    pub fn available_commands(&self) -> &[String] {
        &self.available_commands
    }

    /// JSON Schema descriptors for the terminal tools.
    pub fn descriptors() -> Vec<ToolDescriptor> {
        vec![
            desc(
                "run_shell",
                "Run a command.",
                json!({"type":"object","properties":{"cwd":{"type":"string"},"command":{"type":"string"},"arguments":{"type":"string"}},"required":["cwd","command"]}),
            ),
            desc(
                "run_invoke",
                "Run an invoke command.",
                json!({"type":"object","properties":{"command":{"type":"string"}},"required":["command"]}),
            ),
            desc(
                "list_available_shell_commands",
                "List of available shell commands.",
                json!({}),
            ),
        ]
    }

    /// Dispatch a named terminal tool.
    pub fn call(&self, name: &str, args: &Map<String, Value>) -> Result<CallResult, String> {
        match name {
            "run_shell" => Ok(CallResult::plain(self.run_shell(
                &arg_str(args, "cwd")?,
                &arg_str(args, "command")?,
                &arg_str_or_empty(args, "arguments"),
            )?)),
            "run_invoke" => Ok(CallResult::plain(
                self.run_invoke(&arg_str(args, "command")?)?,
            )),
            "list_available_shell_commands" => Ok(CallResult::plain(format!(
                "available: {:?}",
                self.available_commands()
            ))),
            _ => Err(format!("tool '{name}' not available")),
        }
    }

    fn run_shell(&self, cwd: &str, command: &str, arguments: &str) -> Result<String, String> {
        if !self.available_commands.iter().any(|c| c == command) {
            return Err(format!(
                "'{command}'-command is not available (available command list is {:?})",
                self.available_commands
            ));
        }
        if !cwd_allowed(cwd, &self.cwd_list) {
            return Err(format!(
                "'{cwd}'-cwd is denied for '{command}'-command (allowed cwd for '{command}'-command is {:?})",
                self.cwd_list
            ));
        }
        let cmd = format!("{command} {arguments}");
        run_observed_command(&cmd, Path::new(cwd), self.command_execution_limit)
    }

    fn run_invoke(&self, command: &str) -> Result<String, String> {
        let python = invoke_python(&self.cwd);
        let cmd = format!("{} -m invoke {command}", python.display());
        run_observed_command(&cmd, &self.cwd, self.invoke_execution_limit)
    }
}

fn cwd_allowed(cwd: &str, list: &[String]) -> bool {
    let abs = path_string(&abspath(cwd));
    list.iter()
        .any(|allowed| abs.starts_with(&path_string(&abspath(allowed))))
}

fn path_string(path: &Path) -> String {
    path.to_string_lossy().into_owned()
}

fn invoke_python(cwd: &Path) -> PathBuf {
    #[cfg(windows)]
    {
        cwd.join(".venv").join("Scripts").join("python.exe")
    }
    #[cfg(not(windows))]
    {
        cwd.join(".venv").join("bin").join("python")
    }
}

fn desc(name: &str, description: &str, parameters: Value) -> ToolDescriptor {
    ToolDescriptor {
        name: name.to_string(),
        description: description.to_string(),
        parameters,
    }
}

//! Tool registry for the native harness.
//!
//! Each tool-set lives in its own module (`fs`, `git`, `terminal`, `user`,
//! `skills`, `roles`). The registry is assembled from the conf `tools` object:
//! a missing key skips the module, an unknown name is rejected at load time by
//! `conf`. Agents see a flat list of descriptors and call tools by name.

use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};
use std::sync::Arc;

use logger::Logger;
use serde_json::{Map, Value};

pub mod fs;
pub mod git;
pub mod process;
pub mod roles;
pub mod skills;
pub mod terminal;
pub mod user;

use fs::FsModule;
use git::GitModule;
use roles::RolesModule;
use skills::SkillsModule;
use terminal::TerminalModule;
use user::UserModule;

/// Human-readable description of every tool module and its conf parameters.
///
/// Printed by `harness.exe --help-tools`. Embedded at compile time from
/// `crates/tools/help-user.txt`.
pub const HELP_USER: &str = include_str!("../help-user.txt");

/// JSON Schema descriptor exposed to the agent as one tool.
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct ToolDescriptor {
    /// Tool name (function name in the vendor API).
    pub name: String,
    /// Short description sent to the model (not `tool_help.md`).
    pub description: String,
    /// JSON Schema `parameters` object.
    pub parameters: Value,
}

/// How [`ToolRegistry::call`] should log the result.
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub enum CallKind {
    /// Default: `Result:` + markdown fence.
    Normal,
    /// `read_file`: log `Read Nsymbols`.
    Read,
    /// `write_file`: log `Write Nsymbols`.
    Write,
    /// `apply_diff_patch`: log `Apply patch {tmp}`.
    Patch {
        /// Temporary patch file written for `git apply`.
        tmp: String,
    },
}

/// Result of a single tool invocation.
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct CallResult {
    /// Text returned to the agent.
    pub output: String,
    /// Logging mode for the registry.
    pub kind: CallKind,
}

impl CallResult {
    /// Plain result logged as a fenced `Result:` block.
    pub fn plain(output: impl Into<String>) -> Self {
        Self {
            output: output.into(),
            kind: CallKind::Normal,
        }
    }
}

enum Module {
    Fs(FsModule),
    Git(GitModule),
    Terminal(TerminalModule),
    User(UserModule),
    Skills(SkillsModule),
    Roles(RolesModule),
}

impl Module {
    fn owns(&self, name: &str) -> bool {
        self.descriptors().iter().any(|d| d.name == name)
    }

    fn descriptors(&self) -> Vec<ToolDescriptor> {
        match self {
            Module::Fs(_) => FsModule::descriptors(),
            Module::Git(_) => GitModule::descriptors(),
            Module::Terminal(_) => TerminalModule::descriptors(),
            Module::User(_) => UserModule::descriptors(),
            Module::Skills(m) => m.descriptors(),
            Module::Roles(m) => m.descriptors(),
        }
    }

    fn call(&self, name: &str, args: &Map<String, Value>) -> Result<CallResult, String> {
        match self {
            Module::Fs(m) => m.call(name, args),
            Module::Git(m) => m.call(name, args),
            Module::Terminal(m) => m.call(name, args),
            Module::User(m) => m.call(name, args),
            Module::Skills(m) => m.call(name, args),
            Module::Roles(m) => m.call(name, args),
        }
    }
}

/// Runtime context supplied by the agent loop when building the registry.
pub struct ToolsContext {
    /// Agent workspace (`settings.cwd`).
    pub cwd: PathBuf,
    /// Session log directory used as `temp-dir` for patches.
    pub temp_dir: PathBuf,
    /// Shared session logger.
    pub logger: Arc<dyn Logger>,
}

/// Flat dispatch table of enabled tool modules.
pub struct ToolRegistry {
    modules: Vec<Module>,
    logger: Arc<dyn Logger>,
}

impl ToolRegistry {
    /// Assemble modules from typed conf settings.
    pub fn build(settings: &conf::ToolsSettings, ctx: ToolsContext) -> Self {
        let _ = std::fs::create_dir_all(&ctx.temp_dir);

        let mut modules = Vec::new();
        let (fs_module, sandbox) = FsModule::build(settings.fs.as_ref());
        if let Some(m) = fs_module {
            modules.push(Module::Fs(m));
        }
        if let Some(git) = &settings.git {
            modules.push(Module::Git(GitModule::new(
                sandbox.clone(),
                git.executable.clone(),
                git.execution_limit,
                ctx.cwd.clone(),
                ctx.temp_dir.clone(),
            )));
        }
        let command_timeout = settings
            .terminal
            .as_ref()
            .map(|t| t.command_execution_limit);
        if let Some(term) = &settings.terminal {
            modules.push(Module::Terminal(TerminalModule::new(
                ctx.cwd.clone(),
                term.cwd_list.clone(),
                term.available_commands.clone(),
                term.command_execution_limit,
                term.invoke_execution_limit,
            )));
        }
        if settings.user.is_some() {
            modules.push(Module::User(UserModule::new(sandbox, command_timeout)));
        }
        if let Some(skills) = settings.skills.clone() {
            modules.push(Module::Skills(SkillsModule::new(skills)));
        }
        if let Some(roles) = settings.roles.clone() {
            modules.push(Module::Roles(RolesModule::new(roles)));
        }

        Self {
            modules,
            logger: ctx.logger,
        }
    }

    /// Descriptors of every enabled module, in registration order.
    pub fn list(&self) -> Vec<ToolDescriptor> {
        self.modules.iter().flat_map(|m| m.descriptors()).collect()
    }

    /// Dispatch `name` to the owning module and log the result like Python `DefaultTools.call`.
    pub fn call(&self, name: &str, args: Value) -> Result<String, String> {
        let map = match args {
            Value::Object(m) => m,
            Value::Null => Map::new(),
            other => {
                return Err(format!(
                    "tool '{name}' arguments must be an object, got {other}"
                ))
            }
        };
        let module = self
            .modules
            .iter()
            .find(|m| m.owns(name))
            .ok_or_else(|| format!("tool '{name}' not available"))?;
        let result = module.call(name, &map)?;
        match &result.kind {
            CallKind::Patch { tmp } => self.logger.log_line(&format!("Apply patch {tmp}")),
            CallKind::Read => self
                .logger
                .log_line(&format!("Read {}symbols", result.output.len())),
            CallKind::Write => self
                .logger
                .log_line(&format!("Write {}symbols", result.output.len())),
            CallKind::Normal => {
                self.logger.log_line("Result:");
                self.logger.log_line("```");
                self.logger.log_line(&result.output);
                self.logger.log_line("```");
            }
        }
        Ok(result.output)
    }
}

/// Required string argument. Numbers are stringified (models sometimes send depth as a number).
pub fn arg_str(args: &Map<String, Value>, key: &str) -> Result<String, String> {
    match args.get(key) {
        Some(Value::String(s)) => Ok(s.clone()),
        Some(Value::Number(n)) => Ok(n.to_string()),
        Some(Value::Bool(b)) => Ok(b.to_string()),
        Some(_) => Err(format!("argument '{key}' must be a string")),
        None => Err(format!("missing argument '{key}'")),
    }
}

/// Optional string argument; missing or null becomes `""`.
pub fn arg_str_or_empty(args: &Map<String, Value>, key: &str) -> String {
    arg_str(args, key).unwrap_or_default()
}

/// Read a text file whose path was set by the conf owner (skills / roles).
///
/// Not sandboxed: the agent does not choose the path.
fn read_conf_owned_file(path: &Path, action: &str) -> Result<String, String> {
    std::fs::read_to_string(path)
        .map_err(|e| format!("{action} → cannot read '{}': {e}", path.display()))
}

//! Typed settings produced by the conf crate.
//!
//! Other crates receive these structures and never open json5 files themselves.

use std::collections::BTreeMap;
use std::path::PathBuf;

use crate::error::ConfError;

/// Confirmation behaviour when a session limit is exceeded.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum Mode {
    /// Ask the user before continuing past a limit.
    Safe,
    /// Log the breach and continue.
    AutoAccept,
}

impl Mode {
    /// `true` when limit breaches require an interactive confirmation.
    pub fn is_safe(self) -> bool {
        matches!(self, Mode::Safe)
    }
}

/// Which HTTP agent implementation to construct.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum AgentKind {
    /// xAI OpenAI-compatible chat completions (`grok-*`).
    Xai {
        /// API model id, e.g. `grok-4.6`.
        api_model: String,
    },
    /// Google `generateContent` REST (`gemini-*`).
    Google {
        /// API model id, e.g. `gemini-3.8-flash`.
        api_model: String,
    },
    /// Anthropic Messages API (`claude-*`).
    Claude {
        /// API model id, e.g. `claude-sonnet-5-5`.
        api_model: String,
    },
    /// DeepSeek OpenAI-compatible chat completions (`deepseek-*`).
    DeepSeek {
        /// API model id, e.g. `deepseek-chat`.
        api_model: String,
    },
    /// AliBaba DashScope OpenAI-compatible chat completions (`qwen-*`).
    Alibaba {
        /// API model id, e.g. `qwen-max`.
        api_model: String,
    },
}

impl AgentKind {
    /// Map a vendor + display-name pair to a supported implementation.
    pub(crate) fn from_vendor_name(vendor: &str, name: &str) -> Result<Self, ConfError> {
        match (vendor, name) {
            ("SpaceXAI", "Grok 4.6") => Ok(AgentKind::Xai {
                api_model: "grok-4.6".into(),
            }),
            ("SpaceXAI", "Grok 4.7") => Ok(AgentKind::Xai {
                api_model: "grok-4.7".into(),
            }),
            ("SpaceXAI", "Grok Build 0.1") => Ok(AgentKind::Xai {
                api_model: "grok-build-0.1".into(),
            }),
            ("GoogleAI", "Gemini 3.8 Flash") => Ok(AgentKind::Google {
                api_model: "gemini-3.8-flash".into(),
            }),
            ("GoogleAI", "Gemini 3.5 Flash Lite") => Ok(AgentKind::Google {
                api_model: "gemini-3.5-flash-lite".into(),
            }),
            ("GoogleAI", "Gemini 3.1 Flash Lite") => Ok(AgentKind::Google {
                api_model: "gemini-3.1-flash-lite".into(),
            }),
            ("Claude", "Claude Sonnet 5.5") => Ok(AgentKind::Claude {
                api_model: "claude-sonnet-5-5".into(),
            }),
            ("DeepSeek", "DeepSeek-V3") => Ok(AgentKind::DeepSeek {
                api_model: "deepseek-chat".into(),
            }),
            ("DeepSeek", "DeepSeek-R1") => Ok(AgentKind::DeepSeek {
                api_model: "deepseek-reasoner".into(),
            }),
            ("DeepSeek", "DeepSeek-Flash") => Ok(AgentKind::DeepSeek {
                api_model: "deepseek-flash".into(),
            }),
            ("DeepSeek", "DeepSeek-V4-Pro") => Ok(AgentKind::DeepSeek {
                api_model: "deepseek-v4-pro".into(),
            }),
            ("AliBaba", "Qwen-Turbo") => Ok(AgentKind::Alibaba {
                api_model: "qwen-turbo".into(),
            }),
            ("AliBaba", "Qwen-Plus") => Ok(AgentKind::Alibaba {
                api_model: "qwen-plus".into(),
            }),
            ("AliBaba", "Qwen-Max") => Ok(AgentKind::Alibaba {
                api_model: "qwen-max".into(),
            }),
            _ => Err(ConfError::new(format!("unknown agent: {vendor} {name}"))),
        }
    }
}

/// An agent json5 file found while scanning a directory.
///
/// Produced without reading `api-key` or `limits.USD`. `limits.tokens`,
/// `rpm` / `tpm` / `rpd` and prices are filled so a menu can show the model.
/// Full validation, including the API key, runs only for the chosen file.
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct AgentCandidate {
    /// Absolute path to the agent file.
    pub path: PathBuf,
    /// Vendor string from the file (`SpaceXAI` / `GoogleAI` / `Claude` / `DeepSeek` / `AliBaba`).
    pub vendor: String,
    /// Display name from the file (`Grok 4.6`, ...).
    pub name: String,
    /// `limits.tokens` (`-1` = unlimited). Not the session token limit.
    pub token_limit: i64,
    /// Requests per minute (`-1` = not enforced).
    pub rpm: i64,
    /// Tokens per minute (`-1` = not enforced).
    pub tpm: i64,
    /// Requests per day (`-1` = not enforced).
    pub rpd: i64,
    /// USD per 1_000_000 input tokens (`-1` = free).
    pub input_price: f64,
    /// USD per 1_000_000 output tokens (`-1` = free).
    pub output_price: f64,
    /// USD per 1_000_000 cached tokens (`-1` = free).
    pub cached_price: f64,
}

impl PartialEq for AgentCandidate {
    fn eq(&self, other: &Self) -> bool {
        self.path == other.path
            && self.vendor == other.vendor
            && self.name == other.name
            && self.token_limit == other.token_limit
            && self.rpm == other.rpm
            && self.tpm == other.tpm
            && self.rpd == other.rpd
            && same_price(self.input_price, other.input_price)
            && same_price(self.output_price, other.output_price)
            && same_price(self.cached_price, other.cached_price)
    }
}

impl Eq for AgentCandidate {}

fn same_price(left: f64, right: f64) -> bool {
    left.to_bits() == right.to_bits()
}

/// Fully resolved harness configuration.
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct Settings {
    /// Agent workspace (`cwd` in json5). Tools, logs and invoke run from here.
    pub cwd: PathBuf,
    /// Directory of the loaded `--conf` file (model path is resolved against it).
    pub conf_dir: PathBuf,
    /// Relative log folder inside `cwd`.
    pub log_folder: String,
    /// Agent / model / limits.
    pub agent: AgentSettings,
    /// Enabled tool modules. Missing key = module off.
    pub tools: ToolsSettings,
    /// Conf JSON after vars substitution, with `agent.config` set to the
    /// absolute chosen agent file. Written into dump files as `"settings"`.
    pub source: serde_json::Value,
}

/// Model identity, resolved API key, session limits and model prices.
#[derive(Debug, Clone)]
pub struct AgentSettings {
    /// Vendor string from the model file (`SpaceXAI` / `GoogleAI` / `Claude` / `DeepSeek` / `AliBaba`).
    pub vendor: String,
    /// Display name from the model file (`Grok 4.6`, ...).
    pub name: String,
    /// Already-resolved API key (file contents or the literal string).
    pub api_key: String,
    /// Which agent implementation to start.
    pub kind: AgentKind,
    /// Limit confirmation mode.
    pub mode: Mode,
    /// Session token limit (`-1` = unlimited).
    pub token_limit: i64,
    /// Session iteration limit (`-1` = unlimited).
    pub iteration_limit: i64,
    /// Session USD limit (`-1` = unlimited).
    pub usd_limit: f64,
    /// Requests per minute (`-1` = not enforced).
    pub rpm: i64,
    /// Tokens per minute (`-1` = not enforced).
    pub tpm: i64,
    /// Requests per day (`-1` = not enforced).
    pub rpd: i64,
    /// USD per 1_000_000 input tokens (`-1` = free, kept as `-1`).
    pub input_price: f64,
    /// USD per 1_000_000 output tokens (`-1` = free, kept as `-1`).
    pub output_price: f64,
    /// USD per 1_000_000 cached tokens (`-1` = free, kept as `-1`).
    pub cached_price: f64,
    /// Absolute path of the agent json5 file that was loaded.
    pub config_path: PathBuf,
}

/// Optional settings for each tool module. `None` = module disabled.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Default)]
pub struct ToolsSettings {
    /// Filesystem tools.
    pub fs: Option<FsSettings>,
    /// Git tools.
    pub git: Option<GitSettings>,
    /// Shell / invoke tools.
    pub terminal: Option<TerminalSettings>,
    /// User-interaction tools.
    pub user: Option<UserSettings>,
    /// Skills catalog (`get_skill_explanation`).
    pub skills: Option<SkillsSettings>,
    /// Roles catalog (`get_role_explanation`).
    pub roles: Option<RolesSettings>,
}

/// Settings for the `fs` module.
